{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481879,"nonce":"5XT6dD4FPTaVbo/ynexXIUIiyuk=","title":"Title: `Tailwind v4 generates no variants for hand-written `@layer utilities` classes, so `motion-safe:oto-enter` and `open:oto-enter` are dead class names`","message":"**What is wrong.** `.oto-pulse` and `.oto-enter` are hand-written rules inside a plain `@layer utilities` block in `web/src/index.css:109-168`. Tailwind v4 treats that block as pass-through CSS: it is emitted verbatim and the class names are never registered as utilities. Only `@utility` registers a custom utility, and only a registered utility can have a variant generated for it. There is no `@utility` anywhere in `web/src` and no `tailwind.config.*` — the build is `@tailwindcss/vite` with `tailwindcss ^4.1.18` (`web/package.json:29,34`), and `web/src/index.css` is the only stylesheet.\n\n**The demonstration.** Two call sites apply a variant to one of these classes. Both are dead:\n\n- `web/src/features/settings/TuningSection.tsx:1767` — `motion-safe:oto-enter` on the tuning save bar. The scanner sees the candidate, resolves the `motion-safe` variant, fails to resolve `oto-enter` to any registered utility, and discards the candidate. No `.motion-safe\\:oto-enter` rule is emitted, so the save bar has never had an enter animation.\n- `web/src/components/ui/Dialog.tsx:109` — `open:oto-enter` on the `\u003cdialog\u003e` element, same mechanism. The dialog open transition the code appears to declare does not exist either.\n\nThe bare uses are fine and do work: `StateChip.tsx:91` applies `oto-pulse` with no variant. Every other `motion-safe:` in the tree targets a first-party Tailwind utility and is unaffected — `states.tsx:62` and `AppShell.tsx:56` (`animate-pulse`), `primitives.tsx:87` (`animate-spin`). So the count is: 2 affected sites, both on `.oto-enter`, and 3 `motion-safe:` sites that are correct.\n\n**Why it matters.** These are silent. The class name is present in the DOM and looks intentional in review, which is why the pattern spread from one file to a second. Anything written by copying `TuningSection.tsx:1767` will also be dead. The `oto-enter` transitions are cosmetic, so nothing is broken for an operator — the defect is that the source claims motion the build never produced, and the repo's convention for \"guard this with reduced motion\" is a form that does nothing.\n\nRelated, and worth recording in the same fix: the reduced-motion block at `index.css:140-153` names `.oto-pulse, .oto-enter { animation: none !important }` and then immediately applies `animation-duration: .01ms !important` plus `animation-iteration-count: 1 !important` to `*, *::before, *::after`. The explicit two-class rule is therefore redundant — the wildcard sweep already neutralises any animation, including one on a class that has not been named. Converting to `@utility` does not change that; a new utility does not need adding to the list at `:141-142`.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/index.css:109-168` (the `@layer utilities` block; `.oto-pulse` at `:113`, `.oto-enter` at `:126`, reduced-motion at `:140-153`), `web/src/features/settings/TuningSection.tsx:1767`, `web/src/components/ui/Dialog.tsx:109`, `web/package.json:29,34`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `.oto-pulse` and `.oto-enter` are declared with `@utility` rather than inside `@layer utilities`, both variant call sites emit real CSS, and something fails if a future hand-written utility is given a variant again — a build-output assertion that `.motion-safe\\:oto-enter` and `.open\\:oto-enter` exist in the compiled stylesheet is enough, since the failure mode is absence and no runtime test can see it.","files":null}]}